---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="Jason Deters — Artist | Axiom Atelier">
  <main>
    <header class="artist-hero">
      <img
        src="/images/jason-deters.jpg"
        alt="Jason Deters, artist and founder of Axiom Atelier"
        fetchpriority="high"
      />
      <div class="hero-title">
        <p class="eyebrow">Artist · Maker · Founder</p>
        <h1>Jason<br />Deters</h1>
      </div>
    </header>

    <section class="artist-introduction" aria-labelledby="introduction-heading">
      <p class="section-number" aria-hidden="true">01</p>
      <div>
        <h2 id="introduction-heading" class="visually-hidden">About Jason Deters</h2>
        <p class="lead">
          Jason Deters is an artist and maker working in High Point, North Carolina.
          His practice moves between painting, constructed objects, material
          experimentation, and design.
        </p>
        <p>
          Rather than beginning with a commitment to a particular medium or recognizable
          visual style, Deters begins by asking:
        </p>
        <blockquote>
          What is already present here—and what conditions would allow it to become visible?
        </blockquote>
        <p>The material and method follow.</p>
        <p>
          His work is informed by <strong>Latent Form Theory</strong>, a philosophical
          framework concerned with the idea that meaningful form is often discovered
          rather than invented—that materials, environments, systems, and ideas possess
          possibilities that become visible through observation, restraint, and deliberate
          action.
        </p>
        <p>
          The artist’s role, then, is not always to impose an answer. Sometimes it is to
          create the conditions in which something can reveal itself.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="practice-section" aria-labelledby="material-heading">
      <div class="section-label">
        <p class="section-number">02</p>
        <h2 id="material-heading">Material as Collaborator</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="section-copy">
        <p>Many of Deters’ paintings begin far from the studio.</p>
        <p>
          Earth, mineral deposits, stone, clay, and other naturally occurring materials
          are collected and transformed into working pigments and aggregates. Their places
          of origin become part of the provenance of the finished work.
        </p>
        <p>
          These materials are not selected merely to imitate conventional paint. Their
          limitations matter.
        </p>
        <p class="material-list">
          They fracture.<br />
          They accumulate.<br />
          They fall.<br />
          They resist.
        </p>
        <p>
          They produce colors dictated by geology rather than a manufactured palette.
        </p>
        <p>
          Instead of forcing these materials to behave exactly as expected, Deters
          establishes a set of conditions and allows their physical properties to
          participate in determining the final composition. Material may be thrown,
          dropped, tilted, deposited, fractured, eroded, or allowed to migrate across a
          surface.
        </p>
        <p>
          The resulting marks occupy an uncertain territory between authorship and event.
          The distinction between what the artist made and what the artist allowed to
          happen is intentionally difficult to locate.
        </p>
        <p class="axiom">
          The hand establishes the conditions.<br />
          The material participates in the outcome.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="practice-section" aria-labelledby="theory-heading">
      <div class="section-label">
        <p class="section-number">03</p>
        <h2 id="theory-heading">Latent Form Theory</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="section-copy">
        <p>Latent Form Theory begins with a simple observation:</p>
        <p class="axiom">Not everything meaningful needs to be added.</p>
        <p>
          A material already possesses grain, density, fracture, color, history, and
          physical tendencies before an artist touches it.
        </p>
        <p class="measured-lines">
          A space already possesses proportion.<br />
          An object already contains possibilities.<br />
          The natural world contains structures that existed long before human beings
          developed language to describe them.
        </p>
        <p>
          Latent Form Theory asks what might happen if making became less about imposing
          form and more about recognizing what is already present.
        </p>
        <p>It does not prescribe an aesthetic. It proposes a way of looking.</p>
        <p>
          The resulting work may be minimal or complex, geometric or irregular, quiet or
          confrontational. The common element is not appearance.
        </p>
        <p class="closing-word">It is attention.</p>
        <a class="text-link" href="https://latentformtheory.com">
          Explore Latent Form Theory
        </a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="practice-section" aria-labelledby="single-hand-heading">
      <div class="section-label">
        <p class="section-number">04</p>
        <h2 id="single-hand-heading">The Single Hand</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="section-copy">
        <p>
          Deters’ broader creative practice operates through <strong>Axiom Atelier</strong>,
          an independent studio founded on deliberate making, material intelligence, and
          resistance to anonymous mass production.
        </p>
        <p>The work remains connected to the hand responsible for it.</p>
        <p>
          This principle—the <strong>Single Hand Standard</strong>—does not reject machines,
          technology, or contemporary processes. Deters works freely with traditional
          tools, digital systems, projection, fabrication methods, and emerging
          technologies.
        </p>
        <p>The distinction lies elsewhere.</p>
        <p class="axiom">
          Technology may extend the hand.<br />
          It should not erase it.
        </p>
        <p>
          The decisions, interventions, imperfections, and responsibility for the finished
          work remain traceable to a maker.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="artist-cta" aria-labelledby="work-heading">
      <p class="eyebrow">Selected works</p>
      <h2 id="work-heading">See the philosophy made material.</h2>
      <a class="button primary" href="/collections">View the Collections</a>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  :global(body) {
    background: #f4f1e9;
  }

  .artist-hero,
  .artist-introduction,
  .practice-section,
  .artist-cta {
    width: min(1180px, calc(100% - 2.5rem));
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  .artist-hero {
    position: relative;
    min-height: min(82vh, 860px);
    margin-top: clamp(1.5rem, 4vw, 4rem);
    overflow: hidden;
    background: #dedbd2;
  }

  .artist-hero img {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: center 38%;
  }

  .hero-title {
    position: absolute;
    z-index: 1;
    left: clamp(1.5rem, 5vw, 5rem);
    bottom: clamp(2rem, 6vw, 5.5rem);
    max-width: 48%;
    color: #171715;
  }

  .eyebrow,
  .section-number {
    margin: 0;
    color: #706a60;
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.18em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
  }

  .hero-title h1 {
    margin: 0.65rem 0 0;
    font-size: clamp(4rem, 9vw, 9rem);
    font-weight: 400;
    letter-spacing: -0.055em;
    line-height: 0.78;
  }

  .artist-introduction {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(80px, 0.3fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: clamp(2rem, 8vw, 8rem);
    padding-block: clamp(6rem, 12vw, 11rem);
  }

  .artist-introduction > div {
    max-width: 820px;
  }

  .lead {
    margin-top: 0;
    font-size: clamp(1.7rem, 3vw, 2.8rem);
    letter-spacing: -0.025em;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  p {
    line-height: 1.75;
  }

  blockquote {
  max-width: 760px;
  margin: clamp(2.5rem, 4vw, 3.5rem) 0;
  padding-left: clamp(1.25rem, 2.5vw, 2rem);
  border-left: 1px solid #8d867a;
  font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 3vw, 3rem);
  font-style: italic;
  letter-spacing: -0.025em;
  line-height: 1.22;
}

  .practice-section {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(260px, 0.75fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: clamp(3rem, 9vw, 9rem);
    padding-block: clamp(5rem, 10vw, 9rem);
    border-top: 1px solid #cfc9bd;
  }

  .section-label h2 {
    max-width: 430px;
    margin: 0.8rem 0 0;
    font-size: clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 5rem);
    font-weight: 400;
    letter-spacing: -0.04em;
    line-height: 0.98;
  }

  .section-copy {
    max-width: 650px;
    padding-top: 1.6rem;
    font-size: clamp(1rem, 1.4vw, 1.15rem);
  }

  .section-copy > :first-child {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .section-copy p + p {
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
  }

  .material-list {
    margin-block: 2.5rem !important;
    font-size: clamp(1.6rem, 2.8vw, 2.6rem);
    line-height: 1.4;
  }

  .axiom {
    margin-block: clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 4.5rem) !important;
    font-size: clamp(1.55rem, 2.8vw, 2.5rem);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.025em;
    line-height: 1.35;
  }

  .measured-lines {
    padding-left: 1.5rem;
    border-left: 1px solid #b8b1a5;
  }

  .closing-word {
    margin-top: 2.5rem !important;
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3.75rem);
    font-style: italic;
    letter-spacing: -0.035em;
  }

  .text-link {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-top: 1.5rem;
    padding-bottom: 0.2rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid currentColor;
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .artist-cta {
    padding-block: clamp(7rem, 14vw, 13rem);
    border-top: 1px solid #cfc9bd;
  }

  .artist-cta h2 {
    max-width: 850px;
    margin: 0.8rem 0 2.5rem;
    font-size: clamp(3rem, 7vw, 7rem);
    font-weight: 400;
    letter-spacing: -0.05em;
    line-height: 0.98;
  }

  .button.primary {
    display: inline-block;
    padding: 1rem 1.8rem;
    border: 1px solid #171715;
    background: #171715;
    color: #f7f4ec;
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .visually-hidden {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    padding: 0;
    margin: -1px;
    overflow: hidden;
    clip: rect(0, 0, 0, 0);
    white-space: nowrap;
    border: 0;
  }

  @media (max-width: 760px) {
    .artist-hero {
      min-height: 72vh;
    }

    .artist-hero img {
      object-position: 62% center;
    }

    .hero-title {
      top: 2rem;
      bottom: auto;
      max-width: 75%;
    }

    .hero-title h1 {
      font-size: clamp(3.8rem, 20vw, 6rem);
    }

    .artist-introduction,
    .practice-section {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: 2.5rem;
    }

    .artist-introduction {
      padding-block: 5rem;
    }

    .section-copy {
      padding-top: 0;
    }

    blockquote {
      margin-block: 3rem;
    }
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    *,
    *::before,
    *::after {
      scroll-behavior: auto !important;
    }
  }
</style>
